FILE HANDLING (Real Understanding)

What is file handling?

It’s how your program:

Reads data from files 
Writes data to files 
Stores results permanently 


 Opening a file
file = open("example.txt", "r")

Modes (IMPORTANT)
"r" → read
"w" → write (overwrites ⚠️)
"a" → append
"x" → create new file
"b" → binary (images, etc.)

📖 Reading a file
file = open("example.txt", "r")

content = file.read()
print(content)
file.close()

✍️ Writing to a file
file = open("example.txt", "w")
file.write("Hello bro!")
file.close()

⚠️ "w" deletes old content — beginners make this mistake a LOT.

➕ Appending (safe writing)
file = open("example.txt", "a")

file.write("\nNew line added")

file.close()
💎 PRO WAY (Always use this)
with open("example.txt", "r") as file:
    content = file.read()
    print(content)
Why this is better?
Automatically closes file ✅
Cleaner code ✅
Used in real projects ✅
📚 Reading line by line
with open("example.txt", "r") as file:
    for line in file:
        print(line.strip())